Build the Windows natives with MinGW-w64 when Visual Studio is off.

Ghidra builds its native tools on Windows only with Visual Studio:
CheckToolChain fails without it, and Gradle's own toolchain search puts
Visual C++ ahead of GCC whenever it can find one. Here an empty
VISUAL_STUDIO_INSTALL_DIR (-PVISUAL_STUDIO_INSTALL_DIR=, which also skips
the vswhere search) means "build with the cc and c++ on the PATH" instead.
Gradle's GCC toolchain takes them when they are GCC and its Clang
toolchain when they are Clang. Left to look for "gcc", the GCC toolchain
finds MSYS2's own Cygwin-targeting /usr/bin/gcc in a CLANG64 shell.

The decompiler keys its Windows code on _WINDOWS, which only the Visual C++
branch defined; without it, decompile.exe reads its protocol from stdin in
text mode. pdb.exe needs Microsoft's DIA SDK, so it is left out; the PDB
loader written in Java remains.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments

--- a/GPL/nativeBuildProperties.gradle
+++ b/GPL/nativeBuildProperties.gradle
@@ -49,10 +49,25 @@
 			gcc(Gcc).target(current)
 			clang(Clang).target(current)
 		}
-		if (isWindows(current) && VISUAL_STUDIO_INSTALL_DIR) {
-			// https://github.com/gradle/gradle-native/issues/617#issuecomment-575735288
-			visualCpp(VisualCpp) {
-				installDir = VISUAL_STUDIO_INSTALL_DIR
+		if (isWindows(current)) {
+			if (VISUAL_STUDIO_INSTALL_DIR) {
+				// https://github.com/gradle/gradle-native/issues/617#issuecomment-575735288
+				visualCpp(VisualCpp) {
+					installDir = VISUAL_STUDIO_INSTALL_DIR
+				}
+			}
+			else {
+				// Without Visual Studio, use the cc and c++ on the PATH: a
+				// MinGW-w64 GCC or Clang. A bare "gcc" can find an MSYS2 or
+				// Cygwin compiler that builds for neither.
+				removeAll { it in VisualCpp }
+				def mingw = { t ->
+					t.cCompiler.executable = "cc"
+					t.cppCompiler.executable = "c++"
+					t.linker.executable = "c++"
+				}
+				gcc(Gcc).target(current, mingw)
+				clang(Clang).target(current, mingw)
 			}
 		}
 	}
